      "problem": "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ Some rational numbers are not proper fractions.",
      "worked_solution": "The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in Q$ such that $x$ is not a proper fraction.\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∃x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}) & ≡∀x\\in Q,\\,¬(x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}) \\\\  & ≡∀x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is a proper fraction}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in Q,$ $x\\text{ is a proper fraction}.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ All rational numbers are proper fractions.",
